From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Publish the DP edge before rendering, not after

DKR crashes in __scHandleRDP with sc->curRDPTask null. Twelve hypotheses about
ordering were eliminated one by one -- duplicate edges, SP/DP races, queue
saturation, wrong target task -- and every one was wrong, because the defect is
not an ordering defect at all.

DKR's scheduler carries a watchdog that stock libultra does not have. In
__scHandleRetrace (libultra/src/sc/sched.c):

    if (sc->curRDPTask) gCurRDPTaskCounter++;

    if ((gCurRDPTaskCounter > 10) && (sc->curRDPTask)) {
        set_curRDPTask_NULL = TRUE;
        osDpSetStatus(... DPC_CLR_PIPE_CTR | DPC_CLR_CMD_CTR);
    }
    ...
    if (set_curRDPTask_NULL) sc->curRDPTask = NULL;

Past ten retraces with an outstanding RDP task, Rare's code declares the RDP
hung: it nulls curRDPTask and resets the DP registers, but it does NOT clear
OS_SC_NEEDS_RDP on the task. A DP edge arriving after that walks into
__scHandleRDP, reads curRDPTask == 0 and dereferences null.

That is exactly the state the probe captured at the instant of reception:

    msg=668 curRSP=0x00000000 curRDP=0x00000000 <== NULL
    targeted=0x80125FB0 state=0x00000001 flags=0x00000023

curRDPTask null while the targeted task still carries NEEDS_RDP (state = 1).
Only two paths null curRDPTask, and __scHandleRDP clears NEEDS_RDP when it does.
The watchdog is the only path that leaves this combination behind.

The threshold is counted in RETRACES PROCESSED, not in milliseconds:
gCurRDPTaskCounter is reset in __scExec when a task starts and incremented once
per __scHandleRetrace. Publishing the DP edge after send_dl let a whole render
worth of retraces land in between.

The first draft of this patch claimed a software Glide frame blows the 167 ms
that eleven retraces are worth at 60 Hz. The measurement it shipped with refutes
that -- worst case 60 ms over more than two thousand frames, zero overruns. The
hypothesis was plausible, it explained the symptom, and the fix it inspired
works; three reasons not to check it, and it was wrong anyway.

With the caveat that nearly went unwritten: this build uses the NULL RENDERER.
Those 60 ms are display-list traversal alone, with nothing rasterised. The
measurement refutes the hypothesis for this binary, not for a real Glide render,
which will cost far more and bring the budget question back. That argues for
publishing the edge early on its own merits: not a palliative but the only shape
that survives a heavier renderer. And 60 ms of bare traversal already produces
six retraces per display list -- eleven is not far off, with not one line drawn.

What the run does show: 3238 VIDEO messages against 567 RDP_DONE, about six
retraces per display list -- comfortably under eleven on average and not under it
in the tail. One excursion is all it takes, which is why the crash struck once,
at display list 344 of a run that was otherwise progressing.

Publishing the edge before rendering is safe for exactly the reason the SP edge
already is: the queued task owns an immutable RDRAM snapshot, so the renderer
never reads memory the game may recycle, and presenting to the screen is the
port's business rather than the game's.

Measured on the machine, same ROM, same build otherwise:

    before   display lists 344, then EXCEPTION in __scHandleRDP
    after    display lists 1135 and climbing, 7020 presents, 0 null receptions

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
@@ -268,6 +268,13 @@
